<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<title>{{REPORT_TITLE}}</title>
<!--
  CONTRACT for scripts/build-report.mjs (owned by another workstream).
  This file is the 4-PAGE "Trending" report layout (Executive Summary / Traffic / Core Web Vitals / Browser Performance).
  Its sibling report-cm.html.tmpl is the 4-page "Current Month" layout — pick ONE per render,
  they are two separate template files (not one file with a REPORT_TYPE switch).

  PLACEHOLDERS (plain string replace, each appears exactly once):
    {{REPORT_TITLE}}        - <title> text, e.g. "acme-shop — Trending Report"
    {{FRONTEND_NAME}}        - shown top-right of every page header
    {{ENVIRONMENT_NAME}}     - shown under frontend name; pass "" to omit (CSS :empty hides it)
    {{DATE_RANGE_LABEL}}     - shown top-right under the frontend name, e.g. "Jan – Jun 2026"
    {{FINDINGS_TRAFFIC_HTML}}  - pre-rendered HTML (glue script runs markdown -> HTML, e.g. via `marked`)
                                 for the "## Traffic" section of the analyst notes. "" hides the panel.
    {{FINDINGS_CWV_HTML}}      - same, for the "## Core Web Vitals" section.
    {{FINDINGS_BROWSER_HTML}}  - same, for the "## Browser" (Browser & Device) section.
    {{DATA_JSON}}            - a single JS object literal (valid JSON), assigned to `const DATA = {{DATA_JSON}};`
                               in the <script> block below. Contains ALL chart + KPI + table data, keyed
                               by chart id. Schema (all fields required unless noted; use [] / null for "no data"):

    DATA = {
      kpis: [                                   // rendered as the plain (non-CWV) KPI tiles by this template's
        { label: "Sessions", value: "1.2M", change: 4.3, lowerIsBetter: false },
        ...                                      // own JS. change is a percent, can be null; lowerIsBetter=true
      ],                                         // means a NEGATIVE change is the good/green case. The three CWV
                                                  // KPIs (LCP/INP/CLS) are NOT listed here — the template derives
                                                  // them straight from cwvMonthly's latest point and renders them
                                                  // as threshold-scale metric-tiles, not plain figures (see the
                                                  // "Page 1: KPI row" comment in <style> for why).

      trafficMonthly: {                          // bar-in-cell table, page 2 (matches the live app's User Traffic panel)
        labels: ["Jan 2026", "Feb 2026", ...],    // one row per month
        sessions: [12000, 13500, ...],            // each column's bar width is relative to that column's own max
        userActions: [34000, 36000, ...],
        pageLoads: [15000, 15800, ...],
        pctDesktop: [62, 61, ...],                 // optional: renders a "% Desktop" bar column (thin/outlined style)
      },

      cwvMonthly: {                               // page 1 KPI tiles (latest point) + page 3 status row (latest
        labels: ["Jan 2026", "Feb 2026", ...],     // point) both read this same array; LCP/INP/CLS, one row per month
        lcp: [2200, 2600, ...],                    // ms
        inp: [180, 210, ...],                      // ms
        cls: [0.05, 0.09, ...],                     // unitless
      },

      cwvWeekly: {                                // page 3: two small-multiple line charts (LCP+INP share an ms
        labels: ["W1", "W2", ...],                 // axis; CLS gets its own chart rather than a second y-axis on
        lcp: [2200, ...], inp: [180, ...], cls: [0.05, ...],  // the same canvas — see the page 3 comment in <style>).
      },

      browserPerf: {                              // page 4: grid of up to 6 browser×device panels, ranked by
        panels: [                                  // total visits over the period (see references/queries.md for
          {                                        // the selection policy). null entries render as an empty slot.
            label: "Chrome · Desktop",
            months: [
              { label: "Jan 2026", visits: 12000, lcp: 2200, inp: 180, cls: 0.05 },
              ...                                   // one entry per month, same months/order as trafficMonthly.labels
            ],
          },
          ...                                       // build-report.mjs's `--max-browsers <N>` flag, default 6.
        ],
      },
    }

  The template's own <script> block (bottom of file) reads DATA on DOMContentLoaded and builds every
  Chart.js chart + every HTML component (KPI tiles, metric tiles, bar-in-cell tables, browser panels).
  Markdown->HTML conversion for findings is the glue script's job (this template never runs a markdown
  parser) — vendor `marked` or equivalent there, since bundling it here would just duplicate the
  "no CDN at render time" goal Chart.js already solves.

  Chart bootstrap note: every chart/metric-tile colour is read from the CSS custom properties in
  styles/deck-base (via getComputedStyle) at DOMContentLoaded, never held as hex in this script. That
  is what lets @media print's light-theme token override recolour the charts too — Chrome's
  --print-to-pdf renders with print media active from first paint, so the computed styles the script
  reads at DOMContentLoaded are already the light-theme values when the deck is being turned into a PDF.
-->
<script src="chart.umd.min.js"></script>
<style>
  /* DT Flow, Dynatrace's brand font, served from Dynatrace's own public CDN
     (same URL @dynatrace/strato-components' AppRoot.css loads it from) —
     requires network access at render time; falls back to system fonts if unreachable. */
  @font-face { font-family: DynatraceFlow; font-weight: 400; font-display: swap; src: url(https://dt-cdn.net/fonts/DTFlow-Regular-v1.2.1.woff2) format('woff2'); }
  @font-face { font-family: DynatraceFlow; font-weight: 500; font-display: swap; src: url(https://dt-cdn.net/fonts/DTFlow-Medium-v1.2.1.woff2) format('woff2'); }
  @font-face { font-family: DynatraceFlow; font-weight: 600; font-display: swap; src: url(https://dt-cdn.net/fonts/DTFlow-Semibold-v1.2.1.woff2) format('woff2'); }
  @font-face { font-family: DynatraceFlow; font-weight: 700; font-display: swap; src: url(https://dt-cdn.net/fonts/DTFlow-Heavy-v1.2.1.woff2) format('woff2'); }

  /* ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     Shared deck design layer — pasted verbatim from SCRATCH/deck-base.css.
     Do not hand-edit rules in this block; fix them in the shared source and
     re-paste into both report-trending.html.tmpl and report-cm.html.tmpl.
     ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ */
  :root {
    --dt-bg-base:              #19192c; /* Colors.Background.Base.Default (dark) */
    --dt-bg-container:         #212135; /* Colors.Background.Container.Neutral.Default (dark) */
    --dt-bg-container-subtle:  #19192c; /* Colors.Background.Container.Neutral.Subdued (dark) */
    --dt-text-primary:         #ebecff; /* Colors.Text.Neutral.Default — body text */
    --dt-text-secondary:       #b1b2d2; /* Colors.Text.Neutral.Subdued — secondary text */
    --dt-border-default:       #3b3b52; /* Colors.Border.Neutral.Default (dark) */
    --dt-critical:             #ff999c; /* Colors.Text.Critical.Default (dark) */
    --dt-warning:              #eea83f; /* Colors.Text.Warning.Default (dark) */
    --dt-success:              #6fc3ba; /* Colors.Text.Success.Default (dark) */
    --dt-primary:              #adb0ff; /* Colors.Text.Primary.Default (dark) — brand-accent text role */

    --dt-font-sans: "DT Flow", DynatraceFlow, Roboto, Helvetica, sans-serif;
    --dt-font-mono: "Roboto Mono", ui-monospace, monospace;

    --dt-radius: 9px;
    --dt-space-4: 4px;
    --dt-space-8: 8px;
    --dt-space-16: 16px;
    --dt-space-32: 32px;
  }
  :root {
    --bg-page:                 var(--dt-bg-base);
    --bg-surface:              var(--dt-bg-container-subtle);
    --bg-container:            var(--dt-bg-container);
    --bg-container-emphasized: color-mix(in srgb, var(--dt-border-default) 45%, var(--dt-bg-container));
    --bg-container-primary:    color-mix(in srgb, var(--dt-primary) 8%, var(--dt-bg-base));
    --border-default:          var(--dt-border-default);
    --border-subdued:          color-mix(in srgb, var(--dt-border-default) 55%, var(--dt-bg-base));
    --text-default:            var(--dt-text-primary);
    --text-subdued:            var(--dt-text-secondary);
    --good:                    var(--dt-success);
    --warning:                 var(--dt-warning);
    --critical:                var(--dt-critical);

    --rp-text-micro:   10px;
    --rp-text-small:   12px;
    --rp-text-body:    13px;
    --rp-text-lead:    16px;
    --rp-text-title:   20px;
    --rp-text-display: 30px;
  }

  * { box-sizing: border-box; }

  html, body {
    margin: 0; padding: 0;
    background: var(--bg-page);
    color: var(--text-default);
    font-family: var(--dt-font-sans);
    font-size: var(--rp-text-body);
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }

  @page { size: A4 landscape; margin: 0; }
  .page {
    width: 297mm; height: 210mm;
    /* Centres the fixed-width sheet in a wider browser window. In print the page
       box is the sheet, so auto resolves to 0 and this is a no-op. */
    margin-inline: auto;
    padding: 11mm 13mm 9mm;
    background: var(--bg-page);
    color: var(--text-default);
    display: flex; flex-direction: column;
    page-break-after: always;
    position: relative;
    overflow: hidden;
  }
  .page:last-child { page-break-after: auto; }

  .hdr {
    display: flex; align-items: flex-end; justify-content: space-between;
    gap: var(--dt-space-16);
    padding-bottom: 10px;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border-default);
    flex: 0 0 auto;
  }
  .hdr-left { display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 13px; }
  .dt-logo { width: 132px; height: auto; display: block; flex: none; }
  .brand-rule { width: 1px; align-self: stretch; min-height: 20px; background: var(--border-default); }
  .brand-team {
    font-size: var(--rp-text-small); font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: .16em; text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--text-subdued); white-space: nowrap;
  }
  .hdr-right { text-align: right; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 2px; }
  .hdr-right .kind {
    font-size: var(--rp-text-micro); font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: .14em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--text-subdued);
  }
  .hdr-right .name { font-size: var(--rp-text-lead); font-weight: 600; color: var(--text-default); line-height: 1.1; }
  .hdr-right .meta { font-size: var(--rp-text-small); color: var(--text-subdued); }
  .hdr-right .meta:empty { display: none; }

  .page-title { flex: 0 0 auto; margin: 9px 0 var(--dt-space-16); }
  .page-title h2 {
    margin: 0; font-size: var(--rp-text-title); font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: -.01em; color: var(--text-default);
  }
  .page-title .lede { margin: 3px 0 0; font-size: var(--rp-text-small); color: var(--text-subdued); }
  .page-title .lede:empty { display: none; }

  .content { flex: 1; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: var(--dt-space-16); min-height: 0; }

  .ftr {
    flex: 0 0 auto; display: flex; align-items: baseline; justify-content: space-between;
    gap: var(--dt-space-16); margin-top: 10px; padding-top: 8px;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--border-subdued);
    font-size: var(--rp-text-micro); color: var(--text-subdued); letter-spacing: .05em;
  }
  .ftr .brand-name { color: var(--text-subdued); font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: .12em; text-transform: uppercase; }

  .panel {
    background: var(--bg-container);
    border: 1px solid var(--border-default);
    border-radius: var(--dt-radius);
    padding: 13px 15px 11px;
    display: flex; flex-direction: column; min-height: 0; min-width: 0;
  }
  .panel-title {
    flex: 0 0 auto; margin: 0 0 9px;
    font-size: var(--rp-text-micro); font-weight: 700;
    text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: .1em; color: var(--text-subdued);
  }
  .panel-note { flex: 0 0 auto; margin: -5px 0 9px; font-size: var(--rp-text-small); color: var(--text-subdued); }
  .panel-note strong { color: var(--text-default); font-weight: 600; }
  .panel-row { display: flex; gap: var(--dt-space-16); flex: 1; min-height: 0; }
  .empty-note { color: var(--text-subdued); font-size: var(--rp-text-small); font-style: italic; }

  .kpi-row { display: flex; gap: var(--dt-space-8); flex: 0 0 auto; }
  .kpi-tile {
    flex: 1; min-width: 0;
    background: var(--bg-surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--border-default);
    border-radius: var(--dt-radius);
    padding: 11px 14px 10px;
  }
  .kpi-label {
    font-size: var(--rp-text-micro); font-weight: 700; color: var(--text-subdued);
    text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: .1em;
    white-space: nowrap; overflow: hidden; text-overflow: ellipsis;
  }
  .kpi-value {
    margin-top: 7px;
    font-size: var(--rp-text-display); font-weight: 700; line-height: 1;
    letter-spacing: -.015em; color: var(--text-default);
  }
  .kpi-unit { font-size: var(--rp-text-small); font-weight: 600; color: var(--text-subdued); margin-left: 5px; letter-spacing: .04em; }
  .kpi-change {
    display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: 4px; margin-top: 9px;
    font-size: var(--rp-text-small); font-weight: 700;
    padding: 2px 7px; border-radius: 999px;
    color: var(--kpi-accent, var(--text-subdued));
    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--kpi-accent, var(--text-subdued)) 14%, transparent);
    border: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--kpi-accent, var(--text-subdued)) 45%, transparent);
  }
  .kpi-change.good    { --kpi-accent: var(--good); }
  .kpi-change.bad     { --kpi-accent: var(--critical); }
  .kpi-change.neutral { --kpi-accent: var(--text-subdued); }
  .kpi-change svg { flex: none; display: block; }
  .kpi-sub { display: block; margin-top: 6px; font-size: var(--rp-text-micro); color: var(--text-subdued); }

  .notes-body {
    flex: 1; min-height: 0; overflow: hidden;
    font-size: var(--rp-text-body); line-height: 1.55; color: var(--text-default);
  }
  .notes-body ul { margin: 0; padding-left: 17px; }
  .notes-body li { margin-bottom: 6px; }
  .notes-body li:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }
  .notes-body p { margin: 0 0 7px; }
  .notes-body p:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }
  .notes-body strong { font-weight: 600; }
  .notes-body[contenteditable="true"]:focus { outline: 1px dashed var(--dt-primary); outline-offset: 4px; }

  .mini-stats {
    flex: 0 0 auto; margin-top: 12px; padding-top: 9px;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--border-subdued);
    display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 6px;
  }
  .mini-period { font-size: var(--rp-text-micro); font-weight: 700; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: .1em; color: var(--text-subdued); }
  .mini-chips { display: flex; gap: 6px; align-items: stretch; }
  .mini-chip {
    flex: 1; min-width: 0; text-align: center;
    background: var(--bg-page);
    border: 1px solid var(--border-subdued);
    border-radius: 6px; padding: 5px 7px;
  }
  .mini-chip-label {
    font-size: var(--rp-text-micro); color: var(--text-subdued);
    text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: .07em;
    white-space: nowrap; overflow: hidden; text-overflow: ellipsis;
  }
  .mini-chip-value { font-size: var(--rp-text-small); font-weight: 700; color: var(--text-default); line-height: 1.3; }

  .status {
    display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: 5px; flex: none;
    font-size: var(--rp-text-micro); font-weight: 700;
    padding: 2px 8px; border-radius: 999px;
    color: var(--st-accent, var(--text-subdued));
    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--st-accent, var(--text-subdued)) 14%, transparent);
    border: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--st-accent, var(--text-subdued)) 45%, transparent);
    text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: .06em; white-space: nowrap;
  }
  .status svg { flex: none; display: block; }
  [data-rating="good"]              { --st-accent: var(--good); }
  [data-rating="needs-improvement"] { --st-accent: var(--warning); }
  [data-rating="poor"]              { --st-accent: var(--critical); }
  [data-rating="none"]              { --st-accent: var(--text-subdued); }

  .chip {
    display: inline-block; border: 1px solid var(--border-default); border-radius: 3px;
    padding: 0 5px; font-size: var(--rp-text-micro); font-weight: 700;
    text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: .06em;
    color: var(--text-subdued); background: var(--bg-surface); white-space: nowrap;
  }

  .metric-grid { display: grid; gap: var(--dt-space-8); grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(200px, 1fr)); }
  .metric-tile {
    background: var(--bg-surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--border-default);
    border-radius: var(--dt-radius);
    padding: 12px 14px;
  }
  .metric-tile[data-rating="good"]              { --mt-accent: var(--good); }
  .metric-tile[data-rating="needs-improvement"] { --mt-accent: var(--warning); }
  .metric-tile[data-rating="poor"]              { --mt-accent: var(--critical); }
  .metric-tile[data-rating="none"]              { --mt-accent: var(--text-subdued); }
  .mt-head { display: flex; align-items: baseline; justify-content: space-between; gap: var(--dt-space-8); }
  .mt-abbr { font-weight: 700; font-size: var(--rp-text-title); letter-spacing: .02em; }
  .mt-name { color: var(--text-subdued); font-size: var(--rp-text-small); margin-top: 2px; }
  .mt-value { margin-top: 11px; font-weight: 700; font-size: 26px; line-height: 1; color: var(--mt-accent); }
  .mt-value-tag { font-size: var(--rp-text-small); font-weight: 600; color: var(--text-subdued); margin-left: 7px; letter-spacing: .04em; }
  .mt-scale { position: relative; margin-top: 12px; height: 20px; }
  .mt-track { display: flex; height: 8px; border-radius: 4px; overflow: hidden; }
  .mt-zone { height: 100%; box-sizing: border-box; }
  .mt-zone + .mt-zone { border-left: 2px solid var(--bg-surface); }
  .mt-zone-good { width: 40%; background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--good) 45%, var(--dt-bg-base)); }
  .mt-zone-ni   { width: 30%; background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--warning) 45%, var(--dt-bg-base)); }
  .mt-zone-poor { width: 30%; background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--critical) 45%, var(--dt-bg-base)); }
  .mt-mark {
    position: absolute; top: 0; width: 2px; height: 8px; border-radius: 1px;
    background: var(--text-subdued); transform: translateX(-1px);
    box-shadow: 0 0 0 2px var(--bg-surface);
  }
  .mt-mark-p75 { width: 4px; height: 16px; top: -4px; transform: translateX(-2px); background: var(--mt-accent); }
  .mt-bounds { display: flex; justify-content: space-between; gap: var(--dt-space-8); margin-top: 2px; font-size: var(--rp-text-micro); color: var(--text-subdued); }
  .mt-tail {
    display: flex; justify-content: space-between; gap: var(--dt-space-8);
    margin-top: 9px; padding-top: 8px; border-top: 1px solid var(--border-default);
    font-size: var(--rp-text-small); color: var(--text-subdued);
  }
  .mt-tail b { color: var(--text-default); font-weight: 600; }

  .rp-table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; font-size: var(--rp-text-small); }
  .rp-table th {
    text-align: left; padding: 0 8px 6px; white-space: nowrap;
    font-size: var(--rp-text-micro); font-weight: 700; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: .06em;
    color: var(--text-subdued); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border-default);
  }
  .rp-table td { padding: 5px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border-subdued); vertical-align: middle; }
  .rp-table tbody tr:last-child td { border-bottom: none; }
  .rp-table .num { text-align: right; white-space: nowrap; width: 1%; }
  .rp-table .lead { font-size: var(--rp-text-body); font-weight: 600; }
  .rp-table .path { font-family: var(--dt-font-mono); color: var(--text-default); }
  .rp-table .nil { color: var(--text-subdued); }
  .rp-table tr.current td { color: var(--text-default); font-weight: 600; }

  .bar-cell { display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; min-width: 0; }
  .bar-track { flex: 1; min-width: 44px; }
  .bar-fill { height: 18px; border-radius: 3px; min-width: 2px; background: var(--bar-accent, var(--text-subdued)); }
  .bar-value { white-space: nowrap; font-size: var(--rp-text-small); color: var(--text-default); }
  .bar-track.outlined {
    position: relative; height: 9px; border-radius: 5px; background: var(--bg-page);
    border: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--dt-text-secondary) 40%, var(--dt-border-default));
    overflow: hidden;
  }
  .bar-track.outlined .bar-fill { height: 100%; border-radius: 0; }

  .flex-bars { display: flex; flex-direction: column; flex: 1; min-height: 0; }
  .flex-bars-header {
    display: flex; align-items: baseline; flex: 0 0 auto;
    padding: 0 10px 6px; margin-bottom: 2px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border-default);
  }
  .flex-bars-spacer { flex: 0 0 82px; }
  .flex-bars-col-hdr {
    flex: 1; font-size: var(--rp-text-micro); font-weight: 700;
    color: var(--text-subdued); text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: .06em;
  }
  .flex-bars-body { display: flex; flex-direction: column; flex: 1; min-height: 0; }
  .flex-bars-row { display: flex; align-items: center; flex: 1; min-height: 0; border-top: 1px solid var(--border-subdued); }
  .flex-bars-row:first-child { border-top: none; }
  .flex-bars-row-label { flex: 0 0 82px; padding: 0 10px; color: var(--text-subdued); font-size: var(--rp-text-small); }
  .flex-bars-cell { flex: 1; display: flex; align-items: center; padding: 5px 10px; min-width: 0; border-left: 1px solid var(--border-subdued); }
  .flex-bars-row.current .flex-bars-row-label,
  .flex-bars-row.current .bar-value { color: var(--text-default); font-weight: 700; }

  .chart-body { position: relative; flex: 1; min-height: 0; min-width: 0; }
  .chart-body canvas { width: 100% !important; height: 100% !important; }
  .chart-legend { flex: 0 0 auto; display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 4px 14px; padding-top: 8px; align-items: center; font-size: var(--rp-text-micro); color: var(--text-subdued); }
  .chart-legend .item { display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: 5px; }
  .chart-legend .swatch { width: 10px; height: 10px; border-radius: 2px; flex: none; }
  .chart-legend .dash { width: 14px; height: 0; border-top: 1px dashed currentColor; flex: none; }

  .finding-row {
    display: grid; grid-template-columns: 72px minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: 3px var(--dt-space-16); padding: 9px 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border-subdued);
  }
  .finding-row:first-child { padding-top: 0; }
  .finding-row:last-child { border-bottom: none; padding-bottom: 0; }
  .finding-chip { grid-row: span 2; align-self: start; justify-self: start; margin-top: 1px; }
  .finding-title { font-weight: 600; color: var(--text-default); }
  .finding-evidence { color: var(--text-subdued); font-size: var(--rp-text-small); line-height: 1.5; overflow-wrap: anywhere; }

  /* Light theme: chosen in the preview (toggle) or by render-pdf.sh (#theme=light).
     Print follows whichever theme is active. */
  :root[data-theme="light"] {
    --dt-bg-base:             #f9f9fa;
    --dt-bg-container:        #f2f2f5;
    --dt-bg-container-subtle: #f9f9fa;
    --dt-text-primary:        #2f2f4f;
    --dt-text-secondary:      #3f4060;
    --dt-border-default:      #bfc1d2;
    --dt-critical:            #bb0731;
    --dt-warning:             #855400;
    --dt-success:             #2d6761;
    --dt-primary:             #464cce;
  }
  :root[data-theme="light"] .mt-zone-good { background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--good) 75%, var(--dt-bg-base)); }
  :root[data-theme="light"] .mt-zone-ni   { background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--warning) 75%, var(--dt-bg-base)); }
  :root[data-theme="light"] .mt-zone-poor { background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--critical) 75%, var(--dt-bg-base)); }
  @media print {
    :root { -webkit-print-color-adjust: exact; print-color-adjust: exact; }
    .page { break-after: page; page-break-after: always; break-inside: avoid; page-break-inside: avoid; }
    .page:last-child { break-after: auto; page-break-after: auto; }
    .panel, .kpi-tile, .metric-tile, .mini-chip,
    .bar-fill, .mt-zone, .mt-track, .status, .chip, .kpi-change {
      -webkit-print-color-adjust: exact; print-color-adjust: exact;
    }
    .notes-body[contenteditable="true"] { outline: none !important; }
  }

  :root {
    --rp-series-1: #5eafff;
    --rp-series-2: #c39bff;
    --rp-series-3: #6fc3ba;
    --rp-series-4: #f2a0c8;
    --rp-series-5: #f0c25a;
    --rp-series-6: #9aa4c4;
    --rp-grid:      color-mix(in srgb, var(--dt-border-default) 70%, transparent);
    --rp-axis-text: var(--text-subdued);
    --rp-threshold-good: color-mix(in srgb, var(--good) 70%, transparent);
    --rp-threshold-poor: color-mix(in srgb, var(--critical) 70%, transparent);
  }
  :root[data-theme="light"] {
    --rp-series-1: #1a72c4;
    --rp-series-2: #6f3bbf;
    --rp-series-3: #2d6761;
    --rp-series-4: #b03b78;
    --rp-series-5: #8a6200;
    --rp-series-6: #6b7392;
  }

  /* ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     Trending report — page-specific additions. Everything above this line
     is the shared deck-base layer, pasted verbatim; only what is genuinely
     specific to this 4-page layout lives below, and every colour here still
     derives from the shared :root tokens.
     ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ */

  /* Canvas-facing colour twins. Everything ELSE in this file that carries
     colour (bar-in-cell fills, the sparkline stroke, the .status chips) is
     plain HTML/SVG and references a shared --rp-* token with a bare
     var(--rp-series-1) — the browser's own cascade re-resolves that at
     paint time under @media print, so it needs no JS help at all.
     Chart.js draws to a <canvas>, which cannot take "var(--x)" as a colour
     and has to be handed an actual resolved string. That string must come
     from JS, and beforeprint is the only hook available to rebuild a chart
     for print — but instrumented directly (see ctVar() in <script>): print
     media has NOT been applied yet when beforeprint fires, so
     getComputedStyle() in that handler still returns the SCREEN palette.
     There is no later event that fires after print media applies but
     before Chrome captures the page, so re-reading the cascade cannot be
     made to work. The fix is to stop asking the cascade what theme is
     active and hand the script both answers up front: every canvas colour
     below exists as an unconditional pair, --ct-<name> (screen) and
     --ct-<name>-print (print), neither wrapped in @media, so JS can pick
     the right one by which lifecycle event is calling it rather than by
     which theme it guesses is live. Values are the same screen/print pairs
     as the --rp-series-* tokens above; color-mix() is pre-computed to a
     literal rgba() here rather than left as a mix, so this block does not
     also need the colour-probe workaround (see cssColor() in <script>) to
     read cleanly as a value canvas can parse. */
  :root {
    --ct-axis-text:       #b1b2d2;
    --ct-axis-text-print: #595a7d;
    --ct-grid:            rgba(59, 59, 82, .7);
    --ct-grid-print:      rgba(218, 219, 228, .7);
    --ct-series-1: #5eafff; --ct-series-1-print: #1a72c4;
    --ct-series-2: #c39bff; --ct-series-2-print: #6f3bbf;
    --ct-series-3: #6fc3ba; --ct-series-3-print: #2d6761;
    --ct-series-4: #f2a0c8; --ct-series-4-print: #b03b78;
    --ct-series-5: #f0c25a; --ct-series-5-print: #8a6200;
    --ct-series-6: #9aa4c4; --ct-series-6-print: #6b7392;
    --ct-threshold-good:       rgba(111, 195, 186, .7);
    --ct-threshold-good-print: rgba(45, 103, 97, .7);
    --ct-threshold-poor:       rgba(255, 153, 156, .7);
    --ct-threshold-poor-print: rgba(187, 7, 49, .7);
    /* Same screen/print pair as --bg-container (the .panel background each
       chart sits in), for the opaque-canvas backing fill in <script> — see
       that comment for why a <canvas> needs a painted backing at all. */
    --ct-panel-bg:       #212135;
    --ct-panel-bg-print: #f2f2f5;
  }

  /* The build script's contenteditable patch (build-report.mjs) matches the
     findings-body class attribute verbatim (see its regex), so the markup
     below keeps that literal class name rather than renaming it to
     notes-body. NOTE: deliberately not quoting that attribute the way the
     regex spells it, right here in this comment — the regex runs on the
     whole rendered file, this <style> block included, and a literal quoted
     match sitting in a comment gets "fixed" too. The component itself is
     identical to the shared layer's .notes-body — duplicated below under
     the contract name instead of bending the regex to match a second
     class. */
  .findings-body { flex: 1; min-height: 0; overflow: hidden; font-size: var(--rp-text-body); line-height: 1.55; color: var(--text-default); }
  .findings-body ul { margin: 0; padding-left: 17px; }
  .findings-body li { margin-bottom: 6px; }
  .findings-body li:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }
  .findings-body p { margin: 0 0 7px; }
  .findings-body p:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }
  .findings-body strong { font-weight: 600; }
  .findings-body code {
    font-family: var(--dt-font-mono); font-size: .93em;
    background: var(--bg-page); border: 1px solid var(--border-subdued);
    border-radius: 3px; padding: 0 4px;
  }
  .findings-body[contenteditable="true"]:focus { outline: 1px dashed var(--dt-primary); outline-offset: 4px; }

  /* v1 hid ENVIRONMENT_NAME under the frontend name in its own coloured
     line; the shared masthead's .hdr-right only ships .kind/.name/.meta, so
     environment gets one more subdued line here rather than overloading
     .meta (which already carries the date range on every page). */
  .hdr-right .env { font-size: var(--rp-text-small); color: var(--text-subdued); }
  .hdr-right .env:empty { display: none; }

  /* A slot that fills whatever vertical space its panel has left after the
     title — used for the traffic bar-chart body, which is not a bare
     <canvas> (so .chart-body's own canvas-sizing rule doesn't quite fit)
     but still needs flex:1;min-height:0 to grow inside a fixed-height page.
     display:flex is what actually makes that work: flex:1 only does
     anything on an element whose PARENT is a flex container, and the
     injected .flex-bars is this element's child, not itself — without this,
     .flex-bars sat at its own content height (six rows) and the rest of
     the panel below it was dead space. */
  .slot-fill { flex: 1; min-height: 0; min-width: 0; display: flex; flex-direction: column; }

  /* ── Page 1: KPI row ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
     Three plain kpi-tiles carry the traffic counters (a bare count has no
     verdict to carry). LCP/INP/CLS do have one, so they render as the
     shared .metric-tile instead of a fourth plain figure: a raw "2.4s"
     next to a session count can't say whether 2.4s is good, and the
     threshold-scale tile already says so in one glance, using the exact
     same component the CWV page uses for its own status row. This block
     only trims the full metric-tile down to KPI-row height — dropping the
     name/bounds/tail rows the detail page has room for and shrinking the
     value — the tile's HTML is identical in both places (see metricTile()
     in <script>), only the CSS context differs. */
  .kpi-row .metric-tile { flex: 1; min-width: 0; padding: 11px 14px 10px; }
  .kpi-row .metric-tile .mt-name,
  .kpi-row .metric-tile .mt-bounds,
  .kpi-row .metric-tile .mt-tail { display: none; }
  .kpi-row .metric-tile .mt-value { margin-top: 7px; font-size: 24px; }
  .kpi-row .metric-tile .mt-scale { margin-top: 10px; }

  /* Three analyst-note cards, one per topic. v1 gave each a coloured top
     accent bar (purple/amber/teal) that meant nothing — the shared layer's
     own rationale for dropping per-card colour (see the "Analyst-note
     cards" comment above) applies here unchanged, so the topic is named by
     .panel-title alone. */
  .exec-grid { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr); gap: var(--dt-space-16); flex: 1; min-height: 0; }

  /* ── Page 2: Traffic ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
     Flex-bars rows here grow to fill the whole panel (six months in one
     tall card), so the bar itself is drawn taller than the shared layer's
     18px table default — sized for a compact table row, not a page that
     gives this chart the full panel height. The % Desktop column keeps the
     shared "outlined" (coverage-meter) treatment instead: it is a share of
     a whole, not a magnitude, and FPA's own coverage bars use the same
     thin/bordered style for exactly that distinction.

     .bar-cell itself needs flex:1 in THIS context. The shared layer's
     .bar-cell is normally a <td>'s only child — a block box, so it already
     fills the cell's full width with no flex property of its own. Nested
     inside .flex-bars-cell (a flex row, not a table cell), an unflexed
     child only ever takes its min-content width, so .bar-track's own
     flex:1 had nothing to grow into: every bar rendered at the track's
     44px min-width regardless of value, which is why Sessions read the
     same length in Feb and Jul. flex:1 here is what lets the track — and
     therefore the bar-fill's width:N% — actually see the column's width. */
  /* Six monthly points never filled an A4 page on their own, so the weekly
     trend shares it. Stacked rather than side by side: both panels are
     time series running left to right over the same period, so sharing the
     full page width puts them on a common horizontal axis and lets the eye
     drop straight from a month's bar to the weeks inside it. The table
     takes the larger share for its four columns. minmax(0,…) on both
     tracks is what lets the bar tracks and the canvas actually shrink to
     their row instead of being floored by their content. */
  .traffic-row { display: grid; grid-template-rows: minmax(0, 1.25fr) minmax(0, 1fr); gap: var(--dt-space-16); flex: 1; min-height: 0; }
  .traffic-row .panel { min-width: 0; min-height: 0; }

  .flex-bars-cell .bar-cell { flex: 1; min-width: 0; }
  .flex-bars-cell .bar-fill { height: 28px; }
  .flex-bars-cell .bar-track.outlined { height: 10px; }
  .flex-bars-cell .bar-track.outlined .bar-fill { height: 100%; }

  /* ── Page 3: Core Web Vitals ──────────────────────────────────────────
     v1 crammed LCP/INP/CLS into one bar-in-cell table — three different
     units sharing one row of bars per month, which is exactly the case the
     shared layer's .metric-tile threshold scale exists to fix. Composition
     here: a status row of the three metric-tiles for the latest month (the
     "where do we stand" read, each metric on its own correctly-scaled
     track) sits above three small-multiple line charts for the trend, one
     per metric with its own axis. A shared ms axis for LCP and INP was the
     first attempt, avoiding a second y-axis on one canvas — but LCP's
     ~2,000ms range dwarfs INP's ~200ms one, so on a shared 0–2,500ms scale
     INP draws as a flat line pinned to the axis floor: unreadable, which
     defeats the point of a trend chart. Three single-metric panels (the
     monthly deck's own page 3 does the same) is the actual fix, not a
     second axis on the LCP+INP canvas — a dual-y-axis chart is never the
     right read for two different units either way (see the dataviz skill's
     "one axis" rule). The old monthly bar table is dropped entirely: the
     weekly chart already covers the "how did we get here" story at finer
     grain, so keeping both was two views of the same trend competing for
     the same page. Each chart gets its own dashed good/poor threshold
     lines now that it isn't sharing an axis with a metric those thresholds
     don't apply to. */
  .cwv-charts-row { display: flex; gap: var(--dt-space-16); flex: 1; min-height: 0; }
  .cwv-charts-row .panel { flex: 1; min-width: 0; }

  /* ── Page 4: Browser performance ──────────────────────────────────────
     v1's per-panel 6-row × 4-column mini table needed 9px type to fit six
     panels on the page — below this deck's 10px floor. The fix is
     composition, not a smaller ramp.

     v2 gave each panel one large visits sparkline plus a row of three CWV
     verdict chips. That spent the panel's whole height on traffic, which
     the grid order ALREADY encodes — panels are ranked by visits, so the
     sparkline re-told the reader what the panel's position on the page had
     said first. The page is titled Browser *Performance*, so the height now
     goes to performance: one row per CWV metric, each pairing the shared
     .status chip (the latest month's verdict — colour, shape and word
     together, never colour alone) with that metric's own sparkline across
     the period. Visits survive as a subdued figure in the header, because
     "how much traffic is behind this" is still what decides whether a bad
     verdict matters — it is context for the verdict, not the subject.

     The chip column takes a fixed basis rather than sizing to content, so
     the three chips in a panel — and therefore the three sparklines beside
     them — each start on one x. That is what lets a panel read as three
     parallel trends rather than three ragged rows. */
  /* grid-auto-rows rather than a fixed grid-template-rows: the panel count is
     data-dependent (4 on one tenant, 5 or 6 on another), and naming two rows
     leaves any third row implicit and therefore auto-sized — it then takes its
     content height while the named rows divide what is left, which rendered a
     5-panel deck as two squashed rows above one enormous one. Sizing every row
     through grid-auto-rows keeps them equal at any count.
     minmax(0, 1fr), not 1fr, because a track's default minimum is auto: with
     plain 1fr each row floors at its content height and the grid overruns the
     fixed page. .browser-panel carries min-height: 0 for the same reason —
     three metric rows are taller than the two-line card this grid used to
     hold, and without both the bottom row runs through the footer. */
  .browser-perf-grid { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr); grid-auto-rows: minmax(0, 1fr); gap: var(--dt-space-16); flex: 1; min-height: 0; }
  .browser-panel {
    background: var(--bg-surface); border: 1px solid var(--border-default); border-radius: var(--dt-radius);
    padding: 10px 13px; min-width: 0; min-height: 0; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 8px;
  }
  .bp-head { display: flex; align-items: baseline; justify-content: space-between; gap: var(--dt-space-8); flex: 0 0 auto; }
  .browser-panel-title { font-size: var(--rp-text-small); font-weight: 700; color: var(--text-default); min-width: 0; }
  .bp-visits { flex: none; font-size: var(--rp-text-micro); color: var(--text-subdued); text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: .06em; white-space: nowrap; }
  .bp-visits b { color: var(--text-default); font-weight: 700; }
  .bp-metrics { display: flex; flex-direction: column; flex: 1; min-height: 0; }
  .bp-metric { display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 10px; flex: 1; min-height: 0; border-top: 1px solid var(--border-subdued); }
  .bp-metric:first-child { border-top: none; }
  /* Fixed basis, not content width — see the comment above. 104px clears the
     widest chip the formatters can produce ("INP 866ms"). */
  .bp-metric .status { flex: 0 0 104px; justify-content: flex-start; }
  .bp-spark { flex: 1; min-width: 0; height: 100%; min-height: 22px; display: block; }
  /* One month axis per panel, not one per sparkline: the three sparklines
     share a months array and are already column-aligned on the same fixed
     chip basis, so three identical label rows would be two rows of noise.
     The spacer repeats that basis (and the row's 10px gap) so the labels sit
     under the plot rather than under the chips. space-between puts the first
     and last label at the ends, matching the sparkline's own first and last
     point; the 2% viewBox padding is below the width of a label glyph, so
     chasing it exactly would be false precision. */
  .bp-months { display: flex; align-items: baseline; gap: 10px; flex: 0 0 auto; }
  .bp-months-spacer { flex: 0 0 104px; }
  .bp-months-axis {
    flex: 1; min-width: 0; display: flex; justify-content: space-between;
    font-size: var(--rp-text-micro); color: var(--text-subdued); letter-spacing: .04em;
  }
  .bp-months-axis span { white-space: nowrap; }
  /* The good/poor boundaries behind a sparkline, drawn as the same dashed
     hairline the page 3 charts use for a threshold and reading the same two
     tokens, so a threshold looks identical wherever the deck draws one.
     See the thresholdLines plugin for why filled bands lost to these. */
  .bp-rule { stroke-width: 1; stroke-dasharray: 3 3; }
  .bp-rule-good { stroke: var(--rp-threshold-good); }
  .bp-rule-poor { stroke: var(--rp-threshold-poor); }
</style>
</head>
<body>

  <!-- ── Page 1: Executive Summary ────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="page" data-page="1">
    <header class="hdr">
